WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.690
AI homelab hardware is a set of tradeoffs across CPU, GPU, VRAM, system RAM, storage,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:07.480
networking, and power.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.030
The CPU still matters for general orchestration, data preparation, containers, indexing,

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.985
and workloads that do not fit cleanly on the GPU.

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.610
System RAM matters when models are loaded partially in memory,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.121
when multiple services run at once, or when you work with larger datasets.

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:24.470
Storage matters because models, logs, containers, caches, backups,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:25.915
and experiments grow quickly.

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:29.980
VRAM is often the constraint people notice first because it affects which models and

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.320
context sizes can run efficiently on the GPU.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.200
More VRAM usually gives you more room for larger models, larger context windows,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.520
or multiple services.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.470
Quantized models can reduce memory requirements by representing model weights with fewer

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:41.960
bits,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.520
often with tradeoffs in quality, speed, or behavior.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.830
Quantization can make local inference practical on modest hardware,

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.190
but it is still a design choice, not magic.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.055
There is no single perfect machine.

00:00:52.055 --> 00:00:56.185
Integrated GPUs, consumer GPUs, used workstations, mini PCs, laptops,

00:00:56.185 --> 00:00:59.760
and external GPU setups can all be reasonable depending on the goal.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.982
A low-power system may be excellent for always-on services and embeddings.

00:01:02.982 --> 00:01:06.960
A gaming GPU may be useful for local language models and image workflows.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.905
A used workstation may offer expandability at the cost of power and noise.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:14.360
The right answer is the machine that fits the workload and the environment.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.077
Storage planning deserves early attention.

00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:20.960
Separate space for models, datasets, generated outputs, container images, logs, backups,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.360
and experiment notes keeps the lab easier to manage.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.300
Fast local storage helps with model loading and data processing,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.075
while larger slower storage may be enough for archives.

00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:31.000
Networking should match the access model.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.695
A local-only lab has different needs than a multi-device LAN service.

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:37.375
Good hardware planning is not about chasing specifications.

00:01:37.375 --> 00:01:40.320
It is about reducing friction for the work you actually intend to do.
